// Generated by tools/build_content.py. Part 1: Foundations
export default {"lead": "<p>A database is a collection of related stored data, and a relational database stores that data in tables that are linked to each other by repeating a small, deliberate amount of key data. Everything else in the course builds on that sentence.</p>", "secs": [{"id": "p1-core-terms", "t": "Core terms", "h": "<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Data</td><td>Raw facts</td><td>Each student's individual quiz score</td></tr><tr><td>Information</td><td>Processed data that is useful for decision-making</td><td>The class mean on that quiz</td></tr><tr><td>Database</td><td>A collection of related stored data</td><td>NSFdb</td></tr><tr><td>Integrated database</td><td>Related data used at the same time by many departments and users, stored with as little repetition as possible</td><td>One university database shared by the registrar, advising and billing</td></tr><tr><td>DBMS (database management system)</td><td>The software through which all access to the database passes</td><td>Oracle, IBM DB2, Microsoft SQL Server, MySQL, PostgreSQL</td></tr><tr><td>RDBMS</td><td>A DBMS built on the relational model</td><td>All five above</td></tr><tr><td>Metadata</td><td>Data about data: the description of the database's own structure</td><td>Table names, column names, data types</td></tr><tr><td>Enterprise</td><td>The organization the database serves</td><td>North Shore Fashion</td></tr><tr><td>Structured data</td><td>Data that fits a table with named variables</td><td>An inventory count</td></tr><tr><td>Unstructured data</td><td>Data that does not, usually spoken or written text</td><td>Tweets, product reviews</td></tr><tr><td>Query</td><td>A question asked of the database</td><td>\"Who placed order 608?\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>What a DBMS does: sets up storage structures, loads data, accepts queries, formats results, hides data a user may not see, performs updates, controls concurrent use, and handles backup and recovery.</p>"}, {"id": "p1-two-uses-of-data-two-kinds-of-database", "t": "Two uses of data, two kinds of database", "h": "<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Operational database</th><th>Analytical database</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Manage day-to-day operations</td><td>Make informed decisions</td></tr><tr><td>Typical question</td><td>Can this order be filled right now?</td><td>How have sales of this product trended?</td></tr><tr><td>Data</td><td>Current, structured</td><td>Time-variant, multi-source, structured and unstructured</td></tr><tr><td>Activity</td><td>Constant inserts, updates, deletes (OLTP)</td><td>Mostly read-only (OLAP)</td></tr><tr><td>Design goal</td><td>Accuracy, so highly normalized</td><td>Query speed, so lightly normalized</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>This split comes back in Parts 5 and 6: it decides how far you normalize.</p>"}, {"id": "p1-why-integrated-databases-replaced-separate-files", "t": "Why integrated databases replaced separate files", "h": "<p>Before databases, each application kept its own files. The same data was collected by several departments and never shared, which produced \"islands of information\" that were outdated or contradicted each other. An integrated database fixes this. The advantages the textbook lists:</p><ul><li>Sharing of data</li><li>Control of redundancy</li><li>Data consistency</li><li>Improved data standards</li><li>Better data security</li><li>Improved data integrity</li><li>Balancing of conflicting requirements</li><li>Faster development of new applications</li><li>Better data accessibility</li><li>Economy of scale</li><li>More control over concurrency</li><li>Better backup and recovery</li></ul>"}, {"id": "p1-people-in-the-database-environment", "t": "People in the database environment", "h": "<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>What they do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>End users: naive</td><td>Use menu-driven application programs; never write queries (a registrar's clerk printing transcripts)</td></tr><tr><td>End users: casual (sophisticated)</td><td>Write their own queries in the query language (the registrar asking a one-off question)</td></tr><tr><td>End users: secondary</td><td>Use output from the database without touching it (a student reading a transcript)</td></tr><tr><td>Application programmers</td><td>Write the programs other users run</td></tr><tr><td>Database designer</td><td>Interviews users and builds the conceptual and logical design</td></tr><tr><td>Database administrator (DBA)</td><td>Does physical design and implementation, then manages the operating database</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"id": "p1-the-three-generations-of-databases", "t": "The three generations of databases", "h": "<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Generation</th><th>Years</th><th>Models</th><th>What to remember</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Pre-relational</td><td>1950 to 1972</td><td>Hierarchical (IBM IMS, built for the Apollo program), network (IDS; the CODASYL DBTG model)</td><td>Data linked in fixed hierarchies. Queries had to be planned in advance: a hierarchy with product on top answers \"who bought this product\" fast and \"what did this customer buy\" slowly. The network model allowed links in both directions but kept the same basic problem.</td></tr><tr><td>2. Relational</td><td>1972 onward</td><td>Relational (E.F. Codd's 1970 paper; IBM System R produced SQL; Oracle was the first commercial relational DBMS, 1979)</td><td>Simple tables on a mathematical foundation. Any question can be asked without pre-planned paths. Still the dominant model today.</td></tr><tr><td>3. Next generation</td><td>2005 to present</td><td>NoSQL, NewSQL, Hadoop</td><td>Many models to choose from, built for big data and unstructured data. Adds to relational; does not replace it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li><strong>NoSQL</strong> means \"not only SQL\", not \"no relational\". It covers any data environment that relaxes relational constraints to gain flexibility and scale. Four main types: key-value, column-oriented, document-oriented, graph.</li><li><strong>NewSQL</strong> databases are relational and keep the strict guarantees, while adding capabilities that imitate NoSQL scale.</li><li><strong>Big data</strong> is capturing, organizing and analyzing massive amounts of data. Its five V's: volume, velocity, variety, veracity, value.</li><li><strong>Are relational databases disappearing?</strong> No. Where data must be consistent and correct, as in transaction processing, relational is still usually the best choice.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "p1-controlled-redundancy-the-idea-everything-rests-on", "t": "Controlled redundancy: the idea everything rests on", "h": "<p>Relational databases keep redundancy (duplicated data) as low as possible, but a small amount is required for the model to work. A value that uniquely identifies a row in one table is repeated in a related table to connect the two. The lecture calls this the most fundamental principle in relational databases.</p><p>CUSTOMERS</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Customer_ID</th><th>Full_Name</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Tammy Bryant</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Roy White</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Gary Jenkins</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>ORDERS</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Order_ID</th><th>Customer_ID</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>608</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>766</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Customer_ID appears in both tables. That repetition is the controlled redundancy. Because order 608 carries Customer_ID 3, the database knows Gary Jenkins placed it, and neither table could answer that alone. In Part 4 the repeated column gets its formal name: a <strong>foreign key</strong> that refers to a <strong>primary key</strong>. Every relationship line on an ER diagram is implemented this way.</p>"}]};
